  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nComplex upper pattern per satellite imagery showing main trough\naxis stretching directly overhead early this afternoon. Models \ncontinue to have problems resolving fine details of this system,\nbut general trend is to confine heaviest consolidated \nprecipitation across the south half of the forecast area, \nleaving isolated to scattered showers across the north. Snow \nlevels are hovering right at or just above valley floors as \ntemperatures linger right at freezing, complicating snow vs rain\nforecast. Radar shows broad convergence band across the Lower \nSnake River Plain roughly extending from Pocatello northwest \ntoward Carey. HRRR maintains this band for several hours, then \ndissipates it as upper trough axis continues to slowly creep \nwest. At that time, focus becomes more orographic into the \neastern foothills along I-15 between Pocatello and Idaho \nFalls/Rexburg. These features are highly localized, further \ncomplicating the overall snow forecast through the evening. Have\ntweaked the forecast QPF through tonight above NBM forecast \nvalues, but have spread out the influence of the mesoscale \nfeatures with lower confidence in how and where they will \nresolve. Result is a slightly weaker snow forecast overall, The \nimportant message is that the snowfall within lower elevations \nof the Winter Weather Advisory area may be \"feast or famine\". \nSome locations may see very little accumulation and some \nlocations may overperform. NBM and REFS probabilities still show\na wide spread of accumulating snowfall values, with the \ngreatest potential of accumulating snowfall along and east of \nthe I-15 corridor. Overall, there is little confidence at this \ntime in removing any particular zone from the current Winter \nWeather Advisory areas, especially the Upper and Lower Snake \nPlain zones, given the mesoscale impacts. \n\nAs the precipitation focus shifts to the eastern highlands\novernight, expect winds to increase throughout the Snake Plain.\nGusts 25-30 mph will be common overnight. The winds decrease\nlate overnight into early Saturday, but remain locally breezy\nthroughout the day. Orographic light showers also continue\nacross portions of the eastern highlands through the day\nSaturday, but the bulk of the accumulating snow will be over by\nthen. Transitory ridge tries to build into the region for late\nSaturday into Sunday, but shortwave ejecting out of the next\nPacific low could arrive as early as Sunday afternoon keeping a\nthreat for showers over higher elevations through Monday. Next\nfeature arrives by Wednesday with precipitation spreading across\nEast Idaho mainly focused over higher elevations. There is good\nsupport in the ensemble clusters regarding timing of these\nfeatures. There is some potential to see a ridge building into\nthe intermountain west by the end of the work week and forecast\nperiod, but it may be short-lived as additional Pacific energy\nremains upstream.",
